  "biography": {
    "plain_text": "Sir Leslie Charles Thiess was an Australian construction and mining entrepreneur who built Thiess Bros with his brothers. Raised in rural Queensland, he entered earthmoving with equipment bought by his father. His contracting business developed dams, roads and mines, making him a major figure in the country's substantial postwar development.",
    "html": "<p>Sir Leslie Charles Thiess was an Australian construction and mining entrepreneur who built Thiess Bros with his brothers. Raised in rural Queensland, he entered earthmoving with equipment bought by his father. His contracting business developed dams, roads and mines, making him a major figure in the country's substantial postwar development.</p>\r\n",
    "word_count": 50,
    "confidence": {
      "score": 92,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Full substantive source bodies support the allocated facts."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S2"
    ]
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "A fleet of Toyota LandCruisers on a Snowy Mountains construction site gave Leslie Thiess an opening into vehicle distribution. Impressed by their performance, he sought an Australian franchise; when mechanical problems arose, Toyota supplied parts and sent two Japanese engineers to the site. The vehicles' work on rugged terrain provided a practical demonstration of their capabilities.\n\nAgainst his advisers' objections, Thiess financed a shipment for sale himself. A dealer network developed across Queensland, reaching customers beyond his contracting operations. Rockhampton dealer George Jamieson exhibited a LandCruiser at the local show and sold it to a farmer that day.",
    "html": "<p>A fleet of Toyota LandCruisers on a Snowy Mountains construction site gave Leslie Thiess an opening into vehicle distribution. Impressed by their performance, he sought an Australian franchise; when mechanical problems arose, Toyota supplied parts and sent two Japanese engineers to the site. The vehicles' work on rugged terrain provided a practical demonstration of their capabilities.</p>\r\n<p>Against his advisers' objections, Thiess financed a shipment for sale himself. A dealer network developed across Queensland, reaching customers beyond his contracting operations. Rockhampton dealer George Jamieson exhibited a LandCruiser at the local show and sold it to a farmer that day.</p>\r\n",
    "word_count": 98,
    "confidence": {
